   "page_count": 2,
   "pages": [
    {
     "page": 1,
     "text": "CLINICAL SUMMARY - Pope LLC Clinic\n\nPatient: Kimberly Lawrence DOB: 12/20/1992 Sex: F MRN: 446976\n\nEncounters:\n 2026-02-16 ankle sprain BP 112/73 Wt 134\n 2025-07-18 annual physical BP 118/78 Wt 118\n 2024-12-16 Bipolar disorder BP 113/71 Wt 118\n 2024-05-16 URI BP 113/70 Wt 124\n 2023-10-15 Bipolar disorder BP 107/72 Wt 127\n 2023-03-15 Bipolar disorder BP 114/67 Wt 131\n 2022-08-13 Bipolar disorder BP 109/70 Wt 122\n\nAssessment/Plan:\n • Bipolar disorder (stable x 14.9 yrs; compliant with treatment) - controlled; medications: aripiprazole 15 mg daily. The patient reports maintaining her mood stability for 14.9 years on this medication regimen, with no recent hospitalizations. \n • Ankle sprain - The patient reported an ankle sprain following a minor twisting injury. Examination showed no evidence of fracture. She is advised to use the RICE method (rest, ice, compression, and elevation) and follow up if symptoms persist or worsen.\n • Annual physical - Ms. Lawrence's physical exam was largely unremarkable. Blood pressure was 118/78, weight 118 lbs. Cholesterol levels and other metabolic panels were within normal limits. \n • URI - The patient experienced symptoms consistent with a URI, including nasal congestion and sore throat. She was advised to stay hydrated and consider over-the-counter medications for symptom relief. \n • Social history - The patient reports consuming approximately 8 alcoholic drinks per week. There were no signs of alcohol misuse during the visit.\n\n---\n\nDr. Aimee Martin, Psychiatrist\nPope LLC Clinic\n\nClinical Summary for Kimberly Lawrence\n\nEncounters:\nOn February 16, 2026, Ms. Kimberly Lawrence presented with an ankle sprain after a minor twisting injury. Her blood pressure was 112/73, and her weight was 134 lbs. The patient was given instructions on the RICE method for her ankle injury and advised to return if symptoms persist or worsen.\n\nOn July 18, 2025, Ms. had her annual physical examination. Her blood pressure was 118/78, and her weight was 118 lbs. The physical examination was largely unremarkable, and all vital signs were within normal limits. Cholesterol levels and other metabolic panels were also within normal ranges.\n\nOn December 16, 2024, Ms. Lawrence visited for follow-up on her Bipolar disorder. She reported being compliant with her treatment plan, which includes aripiprazole 15 mg daily. Her blood pressure was 113/71, and her weight was 118 lbs. Ms. Lawrence has maintained stability for 14.9 years and has not required any hospitalizations in recent years.\n\nOn May 16, 2024, Ms. Law"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "rence came in for an upper respiratory infection (URI). She reported symptoms of nasal congestion and a sore throat. Her blood pressure was 113/70, and her weight was 124 lbs. She was advised to stay hydrated and use over-the-counter medications for symptom relief.\n\nOn October 15, 2023, Ms. Lawrence visited for a follow-up on her Bipolar disorder. She continues to be compliant with her treatment plan, including aripiprazole 15 mg daily. Her blood pressure was 107/72, and her weight was 127 lbs. She reports no recent mood episodes and has maintained stability.\n\nOn March 15, 2023, Ms. Lawrence had a follow-up for her Bipolar disorder. She remains compliant with her treatment, which includes aripiprazole 15 mg daily. Her blood pressure was 114/67, and her weight was 131 lbs. She continues to report stability in her condition.\n\nOn August 13, 2022, Ms. Lawrence had a follow-up for her Bipolar disorder. She continues to be compliant with her treatment plan, which includes aripiprazole 15 mg daily. Her blood pressure was 109/70, and her weight was 122 lbs. She reports no recent mood episodes and has maintained stability.\n\nAssessment/Plan:\nMs. Lawrence has a stable history of Bipolar disorder, having been compliant with her treatment for 14.9 years. She is currently on aripiprazole 15 mg daily and reports no recent mood episodes, maintaining controlled symptoms. The patient is advised to continue her current medication regimen and follow up regularly.\n\nFor her recent ankle sprain, Ms. Lawrence was instructed to use the RICE method (rest, ice, compression, and elevation) and to follow up if symptoms persist or worsen.\n\nDuring her annual physical examination, Ms. Lawrence's blood pressure was 118/78, and her weight was 118 lbs. The physical examination was largely unremarkable, with all vital signs and metabolic within normal limits.\n\nMs. Lawrence reported symptoms of a URI, including nasal congestion and a sore throat. She was advised to stay hydrated and consider over-the-counter medications for symptom relief.\n\nSocial history indicates that Ms. Lawrence consumes approximately 8 alcoholic drinks per week. There were no signs of alcohol misuse during the visit.\n\nDr. Aimee Martin\nPsychiatrist\nPope LLC Clinic\n"
